One way to control the arrangement of functionalities is to use linkers with different symmetry and/or connectivity.7 If topology compatibility is satisfied, ordered arrangement of different linkers becomes feasible. In this case, functional groups can be found by single crystal X-ray crystallography. Recently, this goal has been worked towards by copolymerizing multiple topologically distinct linkers to produce isoreticular sets of MOFs with systematically modulated pore architectures.8 However, some potential limitations remain with this strategy. First, it is extremely difficult to introduce more than two linkers simultaneously into one framework, as linkers of distinct size and connectivity tend to form different domains instead of one uniform phase.9 In addition, it requires extra effort to maintain the integrity of certain sensitive functional groups under the harsh solvothermal conditions (i.e. functional group protection and deprotection).10 Moreover, this approach so far has only been reported as successful for soft Lewis acidic metal species $(\mathbf{M}^{\bar{2}+})$ .7,11 The resulting structures usually exhibit relatively weak chemical stability, which limits their applications.
As an inorganic and organic hybrid material, metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) are useful for their tunable structure and function through judicious selection of metal nodes, organic linkers, and the combination and modification thereof.1 Placing two or more functionalities having synergistic effects of various kinds within one framework is particularly interesting for this method’s many potential applications.2 A ubiquitous approach to introduce multiple functionalities into MOFs is one-pot synthesis with mixed linkers.3 One of the representative examples of this is synthesis of MTV-MOFs (multivariate MOFs), conducted by Yaghi and coworkers, in which up to eight linear linkers having the same length but distinct functionalities were introduced into one framework.4 Evidence is presented that linkers of similar length and connectivity tend to be incorporated into one mixed-linker framework, rather than a mechanical mixture of single-linker MOFs. Controlled placement of functional groups is crucial for expanding the functions of MTV-MOFs. For example, these properly juxtaposed functional groups can potentially mimic delicate enzymatic systems. Further efforts were made to elucidate the spatial appointment of functional groups by solid state NMR and molecular simulation. However, it is still a challenge to locate the exact position of each component due to the disordered distribution of functional groups.6
In this sense, the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster is an excellent building unit for MTV-MOFs because of its superior stability and tunable connectivity.12 Nevertheless, $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs based on multiple linkers with different lengths, to the best of our knowledge, have never been reported. Efforts have been made in our group to synthesize mixed-linker $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs starting from any combinations of BDC, BPDC, and TPDC $(\mathrm{BDC}~=~1,4-$ benzenedicarboxylate, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate, ${\mathrm{TPDC}}={\mathfrak{p}}^{-}$ terphenyl- $4{,}4"$ -dicarboxylate) through a one-pot synthesis approach; however, a mixture of known phases (i.e. UiO-66, UiO-67 and UiO-68) is always obtained.13 In one-pot synthesis, the competition between kinetically-favored and thermodynamically-favored products makes it exceedingly challenging to achieve mixed-linker MOF products. In order to synthesize mixed-linker $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs, we propose a kineticallycontrolled synthetic strategy, namely Sequential Linker Installation (SLI). In this strategy, a prototype MOF (PCN-700, $\mathrm{PCN}=$ porous coordination network) with eight-connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}$ clusters is designed. $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters in this MOF are lined up such that the terminal $\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}$ ligands on each $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster pairs can be removed14 to create natural ‘pockets’ for the accommodation of linear dicarboxylate linkers with suitable lengths. There are two type of ‘pockets’ in PCN-700; each fitting a linker with a different length. Satisfying the necessary requirements of adequate flexibility and stability, the SLI strategy has been successfully executed on PCN-700, creating a mixed-linker $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF with three linkers of different lengths. In contrast to the one-pot approach, SLI is a kinetically-controlled process, the terminal $\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}$ coordinated $\mathrm{Zr}$ sites in the prototype MOF are more susceptible to replacement by subsequently added linkers because the activation energy is lower than that of forming a new phase.14b As a result, a kinetically-favored product is generated which would otherwise be difficult to obtain by the traditional one-pot reactions. SLI is a prospective strategy to synthesize MTV-MOFs with functional groups precisely located in the framework. The resulting $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs would be of great significance in terms of practicality because of their stability; furthermore, SLI is performed under mild conditions postsynthetically, which provides a potential venue to introduce sensitive functional groups into robust MOF frameworks.15
In order to obtain the prototype MOF, a topology-guided linker design strategy is adopted.16 The combination of a $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster and a linear linker usually gives rise to an fcu net in which the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster is fully connected by linkers, leaving no room for further linker installation.17 To address this, a bcu net is selected as the target topology for the prototype MOF.18 A bcu net is composed of 8-connected cubic nodes which can be viewed as a deduction from an fcu net by deleting four linkers in the equatorial plane of the octahedral $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster (Figure 1). These uncoordinated sites in a bcu net are well poised for subsequent linker installation. However, the reactions between ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ ions and linear linkers such as BDC, BPDC and TPDC always yield a 12-connected fcu net (UiO66, UiO-67 and UiO-68 respectively) under solvothermal conditions, probably because a high-connectivity network is thermodynamically favored compared to the lowconnectivity ones. It has been well demonstrated that MOF topology can be tuned by the linker configuration; therefore we expect that a bcu structure could be obtained through judicious linker design.19 We reasoned that the bcu net could be realized if the two carboxylate groups of the linear linker were twisted off the coplanar position (Figure 2b). As shown in Figure 2c, the two carboxylate groups of the linker are required to be in the same plane in order to form a 12- connected fcu structure. With the twisted linker, a bcu net (Figure 2f) should be expected as opposed to an fcu net (Figure 2e). To verify our hypothesis, two methyl groups were introduced at 2 and $2^{\prime}$ positions of BPDC linker. Since the dihedral angle of biphenyl rings in BPDC is known to be directly related to the size and nature of substituents on the 2 and $2^{\prime}$ positions, the $\mathbf{Me}_{2}$ -BPDC ( $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -dimethylbiphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}-}$ dicarboxylate) is expected to provide two off-plane carboxylate groups.19b As expected, reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}$ -BPDC and $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ in $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) at $120~^{\circ}C$ yielded crystals of the desired MOF with 8-connected topology, designated as PCN-700.
Single crystal X-ray diffraction study reveals that PCN-700 crystalizes in the tetragonal crystal system with a $P4_{2}/m m c$ space group. Each $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster is connected to eight $\mathbf{Me}_{2}.$ 一 BPDC linkers above and below the equatorial plane and eight terminal $\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}$ in the equatorial plane (Figure 1b). The space between two adjacent $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters in the equatorial plane is a natural ‘pocket’ for the accommodation of one linear dicarboxylate linker by replacing the terminal OH$/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ via an acid-base reaction. There are two pockets of different sizes in PCN-700 (Figure 3, pocket A and pocket B), which make it possible to sequentially install two linkers of different lengths. BDC and $\mathbf{Me}_{2}$ -TPDC $\mathrm{(Me_{2}-T P D C=~}2^{\prime},5^{\prime}.$ dimethy-terphenyl- $4{,}4"$ -dicarboxylate) were selected based on our observation that the lengths of linkers $\mathrm{\tilde{\it6.9\mathrm{\AA}}}$ and 15.2 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ for BDC and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}$ -TPDC, respectively) well match the distances between the nearest oxygen of adjacent $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters 1 $(16.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for pocket A and $7.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for pocket B, respectively). Meanwhile, the dihedral angle of biphenyl rings is flexible (from $78.1^{\circ}$ to $89.5^{\circ}.$ ), which endows the framework with certain flexibility and allow the structure to self-adjust according to the linkers.20 In addition, PCN-700 is highly stable; it retains single crystallinity during the subsequent linker installation under solvothermal conditions, which enables us
